`registration_lock` is a rwsem taken for read on every ioctl and open, with
user-space copies done under it in `ctlv_op_exec` (`copy_from_user` of the request).
A faulting `copy_from_user` on a userfaultfd/FUSE-backed buffer can therefore hold
the read lock arbitrarily long, and `ctlv_destroy()` blocks on the write side
(and, because rwsem writers block new readers, all new ioctls and opens block
too).  The code comment about not making destruction wait covers the output
copy, but the input copy and blob handling in `ctlv_op_call` still run under the
lock.  Needs either a snapshot copy before taking the lock or a bounded design
and an explanation.

